INT. GRANDMOTHER'S ATTIC - EVENING

Dust motes in slanted light. Steamer trunks, a dress form, a rocking horse missing one ear.

HAZEL, 12, rummages through a trunk with fierce concentration. Behind her, her little brother GUS, 6, hides under a sheet draped over a chair. A third figure, their cousin RAFAELA, 14, leans in the doorway with her arms folded.

                    HAZEL
(to herself)
It has to be here.

She pulls out a lace shawl. Tosses it. A feather boa. A sock.

                    RAFAELA
Grandma said not to touch anything.

                    HAZEL
She said not to break anything.

                    RAFAELA
Same thing.

                    HAZEL
It really isn't.

The sheet rustles. Gus peeks out.

                    GUS
(to all of them)
I'm a ghost.

                    HAZEL
You're a pest.

                    GUS
Boo.

                    RAFAELA
Gus, come out of there.

He doesn't. She steps over. Lifts a corner of the sheet. He squeals and swats her hand away.

Hazel finds it. A small tin box, rusted at the hinge. She holds it up to the light, breathing hard.

                    HAZEL
Got it.

                    RAFAELA
Don't open it.

                    HAZEL
Why not?

                    RAFAELA
Because she'll know.

                    HAZEL
(muttering)
She already knows everything.

Hazel pries it open. Inside, folded neatly, a stack of old letters tied with a faded ribbon. She lifts the top one. Reads the first line. Her eyes widen.

She looks up. Gus is silent under his sheet. Rafaela has stopped moving.

From downstairs, a voice calls up, warm and unhurried.

                    GRANDMA (O.S.)
Children? Dinner!

All three freeze. Hazel snaps the tin shut.
